WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.400
I've always been a radical.

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.340
I've always challenged the status quo.

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:04.580
Do you feel like you're part

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.175
of a libertarian movement?

00:00:06.175 --> 00:00:07.120
No, I don't, no.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.529
So what are you then?

00:00:08.529 --> 00:00:10.370
Well, I'm not a part of a movement.

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:12.120
I've never been a part of movement.

00:00:14.388 --> 00:00:15.680
I just do my own thing.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:17.640
Walter Williams, the free market scholar

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.580
and iconoclast died last
week at the age of 84.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:23.520
He was the author of 13 books

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.790
and was best known for
his work demonstrating

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:28.300
that government interference
in the free market

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:31.680
has been especially
harmful to black Americans.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.310
Born in 1936, Williams grew
up in the Richard Allen Homes,

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.440
one of Philadelphia's
first housing projects.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.100
I broke out of the North
Philadelphia ghetto

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.550
nearly 30 years ago and
so did most of my friends.

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:44.830
When he was a small child,

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:46.860
his father deserted his family.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.780
He was brought up by his
mother, a high school dropout,

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.760
and the family spend time on welfare

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.810
but Williams would
later draw a distinction

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:55.500
between material poverty

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.950
and what he called poverty of the spirit.

00:00:57.950 --> 00:00:59.540
Even though we grew up poor,

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.360
we didn't consider ourselves
poor, that is a matter of fact.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:05.080
During those days to call
somebody poor was an insult.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:08.220
When I was a kid, my mother
used to always say to us,

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.310
she says, "You know, we
have a beer pocket book,

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:12.000
but we have champagne taste."

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.860
Williams worked as a taxi driver

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.470
in the City of Brotherly love

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:17.390
and was once ordered out of his cab

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:20.090
by a white police officer, beaten up

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:22.530
and then charged with disorderly conduct.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:23.920
He was drafted into the army

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.610
and while stationed in Georgia
kept getting in trouble,

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.320
occasionally with his fists,

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.210
for standing up to the
racism of white officers.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:32.170
But he came to believe

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:34.230
that discrimination should be legal,

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:37.370
just as long as it didn't
involve government resources.

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:40.670
One of my strong values
is freedom of association.

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:44.200
If you believe in freedom of
association, you have to accept

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.800
that people will associate in
ways that you find offensive.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:51.350
And I believe people have
the right to discriminate

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:52.940
on any basis they want,

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:55.510
so long as they're not
using the government.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:58.370
For me, discrimination is
just simply an act of choice.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:00.740
After the army, Williams studied economics

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:02.300
at California State College,

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.600
and then went to UCLA for graduate school

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:07.700
where he was exposed to titans
of free market economics,

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.070
including Milton Friedman, James Buchanan

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:13.110
and UCLA's department
chair, Armen Alchian.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:15.450
Flunking economic theory
the first time around,

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:16.283
he wrote in his memoir,

00:02:16.283 --> 00:02:18.740
"I later realized did have a benefit.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:21.030
It convinced me that UCLA professors

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:22.980
didn't care anything about my race.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.920
They'd flunk me just as
they'd flunk anyone else

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.140
who didn't make it the grade."

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.960
I don't judge people by colors.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.940
I say that well, gee,
you're a man just like I am.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:35.500
And that's what my stepfather used to say.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:37.340
He says, Every man stands up to piss

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:39.460
and he's whatever he is after that.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:41.760
In 1977, Williams started writing

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.370
a weekly column which
was eventually syndicated

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:46.420
in 140 newspapers.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:48.830
He was also a contributor
to Reason magazine

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:52.130
and served as a trustee on the
board of Reason Foundation,

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:54.760
the nonprofit that publishes Reason TV.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:57.010
For all his individual accomplishments,

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:59.510
Williams was especially proud of his role

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:02.880
in making George Mason
University's economics department

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.170
a home for free market radicals.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:07.730
There was considerable
hostility towards our department

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:09.460
and I had a lot of difficulty.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:11.166
And so I just said, well,

00:03:11.166 --> 00:03:13.100
the only way I'm gonna
improve the department

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:15.080
is to try to privatize the department

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:16.720
and actually go out and raise money.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.500
Last week, his former colleagues

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:21.637
at George Mason paid tribute
to their mentor and friend.

00:03:21.637 --> 00:03:24.890
"GMU Econ has lost an
iconic and heroic figure."

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:25.867
wrote Pete Boettke.

00:03:25.867 --> 00:03:28.400
"He taught with with and passion the logic

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:29.527
of economic reasoning."

00:03:29.527 --> 00:03:32.420
"He is one of my few
heroes." wrote Don Boudreaux.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:37.420
Liberty is the rare state of
affairs in mankind's history.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:39.760
Arbitrary abuse and control by others

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.900
is the standard dish, even now.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:46.000
And all the tendencies are,
is for us to have greater

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.923
and greater amounts of our
liberty usurped by government.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:53.563
Rest in peace, Walter Williams.

